// `#[effectful]` says a function that answers a value also writes its argument, so discarding the
// value is an ordinary call. On a function that answers nothing there is no value to discard and
// so nothing for it to say, and a no-op attribute reads as a fact that was accepted -- the same
// reason `#[at_least_one]` is refused on a signature with no variadic parameter and
// `#[when_unmatched]` is refused without its message.
#[effectful]
fn note(x: int) {
    print(x)
}

fn main() {
    note(1)
}
